<Project>

  <!--
    The language version is set here, once, and deliberately.

    Every project used to carry <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>, which made the language
    version ambient: it resolved to whatever SDK happened to be installed on the machine doing
    the build. That is how two CS1501 breaks reached CI on 2026-07-29 having compiled clean
    locally - the developer box was on SDK 10 (C# 14) and CI was pinned to SDK 9 (C# 13), and
    C# 14's first-class span conversions silently rebound a net472 call to a different overload.

    A fixed number turns that class of defect into a build error on the machine that is behind,
    naming the version it needs, instead of a green build that fails somewhere else. Raising it
    is a commit, not an SDK install.

    Why 13.0 and not 14.0. C# 14's first-class span conversions change overload resolution on
    calls that were previously unambiguous, and Jaunty has two known casualties:

      - net472: string.Contains(string, StringComparison) rebinds to the MemoryExtensions
        span overload, which does not exist on net472 (fixed at the call sites, 2026-07-29).
      - Jaunty.Fluent: for an *array* receiver, ids.Contains(p.Id) rebinds from
        Enumerable.Contains to MemoryExtensions.Contains(ReadOnlySpan<T>, T), with the array
        wrapped in ReadOnlySpan<T>.op_Implicit. WhereExpressionVisitor recognised neither the
        declaring type nor the static form, so it fell through to the constant-evaluation
        fallback and threw "variable 'p' ... is not defined" (fixed 2026-07-29).

    Note what the pin did NOT do for the second one. Expression trees are built by whoever
    writes the lambda, so a consumer compiling with C# 14 sent that shape to the shipped
    Jaunty.Fluent no matter what this file says. The pin turned four tests green while the
    product stayed broken; the visitor is fixed now, and the tests that cover it build the C# 14
    tree by hand so they hold under this pin. A LangVersion pin is protection against ambient
    SDK drift in *our* build. It is never protection for the shipped product.

    A repo-wide compiler scan on 2026-07-29 found zero C# 14 features in use, so 13.0 costs
    nothing today. Raising it to 14.0 is no longer blocked, but it is its own decision - see
    docs/specs/010-net10-migration/010-spec.md.

    Requires the .NET 10 SDK, which CI installs in .github/workflows/{ci,release}.yml; the SDK
    version and the language version are deliberately independent.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>13.0</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
